{
"slots": {
"question": [
{
"claim": "The paper investigates whether online political hostility on X/Twitter is associated with political engagement and its antecedents, rather than solely with socioeconomic marginalization or personality traits.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "In this article, we examine a different possibility. Classical research on political behavior emphasizes that education, internal efficacy, political interest, political knowledge, and participation are central to democratic engagement... Yet even classical theories recognized that engagement could become excessive... and more recent work on motivated reasoning and political polarization suggests that politically engaged citizens may also be more likely to process information in biased or antagonistic ways.",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "We extend this revisionist perspective to online political hostility by asking whether hostile political expression on X/Twitter is associated not only with deprivation or personality-based explanations, but also with political engagement and its antecedents.",
"page": 2
}
]
}
],
"central_claim": [
{
"claim": "Online political hostility is positively associated with various indicators of political engagement and its antecedents, including education, internal efficacy, political interest, political knowledge, and participation.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Online political hostility is positively associated with these indicators, with the strongest associations for political interest, political knowledge, and online participation.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "Building on classical theories of political engagement [17,19] and recent work on the darker sides of engagement, we find that online political hostility is positively associated with several indicators of political engagement and its antecedents. These include distal antecedents such as education and internal efficacy, motivational and cognitive indicators such as political interest and political knowledge, and more direct indicators such as online and offline participation.",
"page": 15
}
]
}
],
"evidence": [
{
"claim": "The study uses a dataset combining panel survey data on political engagement with behavioral data scraped from X/Twitter for American users between 2019 and 2021.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Combining two years of survey and behavioral data from X/Twitter, we examine associations between online political hostility and indicators of political engagement and its antecedents among American users.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "We rely on a data infrastructure combining panel survey data with online behavioral data from X... The scraping of X commenced on March 4, 2019 and stopped in January 2021. During this period, we managed to scrape 9,250,180 posts from our panelists.",
"page": 6
}
]
}
],
"key_findings": [
{
"claim": "Online political hostility is positively associated with political interest, political knowledge, and online participation, with these associations being stronger for the primary measure of political hostility than for general toxicity measures.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The associations with online participation and political interest are, for example, 0.236 (95% CI [0.186; 0.285]) and 0.306 (95% CI [0.247; 0.366]) for online political hostility, whereas they are 0.176 (95% CI [0.103; 0.249]) and 0.170 (95% CI [0.086; 0.253]) for AFINN scores and 0.267 (95% CI [0.212; 0.322]) and 0.239 (95% CI [0.170; 0.308]) for Perspective API scores.",
"page": 11
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The association between political engagement and online hostility is stronger among users with high levels of leader-directed affective polarization, particularly for online and offline participation.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Formal interaction models (Fig 3) show the clearest support for online and offline participation, while the evidence is weaker or inconsistent for more distal antecedents such as education... of the eighteen interaction terms, eight remain significant after a Benjamini–Hochberg correction, and these are concentrated among the participatory engagement indicators.",
"page": 13
},
{
"text": "The descriptive pattern suggests that the association between engagement and hostility is often stronger among more affectively polarized users: for those low on affective polarization only four out of eighteen associations, across our three measures of online hostility, are significant, whereas for those high on affective polarization fourteen out of eighteen are significant.",
"page": 13
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Within-person increases in online political engagement are followed by higher levels of online political hostility, suggesting a temporal association, although the relationship may be reciprocal.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "In brief, within-person increases in online political engagement are followed by higher levels of online political hostility across the three hostility measures. We also observe some evidence of the reverse temporal association, in which hostility is followed by higher political engagement, although this pattern is weaker and less consistent.",
"page": 14
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Users who express more online political hostility are more active on X/Twitter, posting more frequently and having more followers and friends, indicating greater potential reach.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The count-model results indicate that higher political hostility is associated with more posts and with larger numbers of followers and, to a lesser extent, friends. A change from the mean level of hostility to 1.5 standard deviations above the mean is associated with an increase in the number of posts from around 420 to around 900 and an increase in the number of followers from around 220 to around 370.",
"page": 15
},
{
"text": "Users who express more online political hostility also post more frequently and have more friends and followers. This suggests that hostile political expression may be more visible on X/Twitter not only because hostile content attracts attention, but also because users who express such hostility are more active and have greater potential reach.",
"page": 15
}
]
}
],
"scope_and_limits": [
{
"claim": "The study's findings are observational and do not establish causal relationships between political engagement and hostility, nor do they generalize to other social media platforms.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The analyses are observational. The RI-CLPM analyses reduce concerns about stable time-invariant confounding by focusing on within-person change, but they do not rule out time-varying confounding, reciprocal relationships, or measurement error.",
"page": 16
},
{
"text": "First, the data come from American X/Twitter users. This platform is a public, textbased, and highly political platform, and the associations observed here may not generalize directly to platforms with different affordances, audiences, moderation systems, or norms of interaction.",
"page": 16
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The measure of affective polarization used captures feelings toward party elites rather than ordinary party supporters, limiting the interpretation of the conditional effects.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Fifth, due to data availability, our measure of affective polarization captures feelings toward party elites rather than toward parties as institutions or ordinary party supporters, as is standard [22]. The moderation analyses should therefore be interpreted as evidence about leader-directed affective polarization.",
"page": 16
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The study does not directly test personality-based accounts centered on Dark Triad traits or sadism, as these were not measured in the survey.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Finally, while we argue that political engagement is associated with political hostility over and beyond personality, as highlighted in prior research, the utilized Big Five controls do not directly test personality-based accounts centered on more antisocial traits such as Dark Triad traits, sadism, or other antagonistic traits. Such traits were not not measured in the survey.",
"page": 16
}
]
}
],
"theoretical_positioning": [
{
"claim": "The authors extend the revisionist perspective on the negative sides of political engagement, which has been applied to misinformation, to the study of online political hostility.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Prior research has only to a limited extent applied this revisionist perspective om political engagement to politics on social media and mainly in the context of misinformation sharing [53]. Here, we extend and integrate the study of the negative side of political engagement into the study of online politics with a specific focus on online political hostility.",
"page": 4
}
]
}
],
"interlocutor_claim": [
{
"claim": "The paper disputes the exclusive reliance on socioeconomic marginalization and personality-based explanations for online hostility by arguing that political engagement is also a key correlate.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Existing explanations of online hostility can be grouped into at least two distinct literatures. One set of accounts emphasizes socioeconomic marginalization, status threat, and grievance... A second set of accounts emphasizes personality and ­emotion-regulation... In this article, we examine a different possibility.",
"page": 2
}
]
}
]
}
}
